(Bug #58: `a buffer called b is environment's "NAME".` copied the bytes in
 correctly and then lost the name's type. The declaration says buffer, and
 LANGUAGE.md:3286 says a statically-typed variable reports the type "the
 compiler knows ... from the declaration" - but codegen re-read the type off
 the initializer's shape, saw an environment read, and re-labelled the name
 as text. Every later read then dispatched as text: `Print b` printed the
 buffer header as a C string (an empty line) and `b's size` fell through to
 the file fallback and answered -1.

 The sizes are compared against a two-step control - `a text called ...`
 then `a buffer called ... is <that text>` - rather than against a fixed
 number, because HOME and PATH differ per machine. The control took the
 already-correct path, so an equal size is the whole claim: the one-step
 declaration must reach the same buffer the two-step one does.)

a buffer called direct is environment's "PATH".
a text called indirect is environment's "PATH".
a buffer called control is indirect.

Print direct's type.
If direct's size is equal to control's size then,
    print "set: sizes agree".
Otherwise,
    print "set: MISMATCH".
Print direct's empty.

(An unset name is the empty text, so the buffer is empty - not -1 long.)
a buffer called missing is environment's "VOX_BUG_58_DEFINITELY_UNSET".
Print missing's size.
Print missing's empty.
Print missing's type.

(A buffer that kept its type still takes the buffer verbs.)
append "tail" to missing.
Print missing.
Print missing's size.
